    {
      "type": "record",
      "address": "package::make-packages-match-their-source::contract",
      "path": ["packages", "make-packages-match-their-source", "contract"],
      "payload": {
        "kind": "package-contract",
        "package": "make-packages-match-their-source",
        "owns": [
          "reading package-set and partition identity from a caller-selected descriptor",
          "compiling a caller-selected package source successor plan through package-loader",
          "building one scoped atomic package-delta request per package",
          "running a caller-declared list of (package, place) jobs, isolating failure to the job",
          "submitting each request through a caller-supplied target client",
          "forwarding a caller-selected signed-package pointer set to the runtime's existing package-file installer",
      "receipt cursor and operation/package-version readback",
      "deriving product-correction completion from the intended target's active package revision and durable matching behavior evidence",
      "refusing change-set completion until every named package and package-set DATA revision is active on the same target"
        ],
        "does_not_own": [
          "choosing which packages, places, package sets or partitions",
          "discovering packages on a filesystem, which belongs to observed-content",
          "engine address discovery or transport basis negotiation",
          "credentials, spend, public/customer effects, or production deployment authority",
          "package compiler internals or process admission policy",
          "publisher trust, package-file verification, package compatibility or package-change admission",
          "repair outside the selected package current-version closure"
        ],
        "composition": "Consumers mount an existing transport owner and pass its target client. This package never imports another package source.",
        "why_this_name": "The previous name, update-one-package-in-a-running-app, welded a quantity and a deployment topology into an identity, and the signature grew to fit it: one package, one app. Neither was a property of the work. How many packages is DATA, and where an engine runs is an address, which is also DATA. A name carrying a count or a place silently specifies behaviour that nobody chose."
      }
    },
